# Python bytecode
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Quantitative model run outputs. These are rewritten on every solve and one of
# them, calibration_distribution1.dat, is 67 MB, so tracking them adds that much
# to history on every round for a file nothing reads across runs. They are
# reproduced by running the model, which is what the replication package asks
# the reader to do; only calibration_state.dat is worth keeping and it is the
# resume checkpoint, kept because this container is ephemeral.
code/*.dat
code/*baseline_benchmark.txt
!code/calibration_state.dat
# The newborn parent shares are the other half of the resume state. They are
# built by the AGGREGATION, which runs after the distribution, so a fresh
# process's first distribution uses the declared default of all clean housed
# parents -- the UNPENALISED draw -- and the intergenerational channel starts
# switched off. Two container rebuilds cost a loop each to that before this
# exception was added.
!code/newborn_parent_share.dat

# The supervisor's own running output. progress_reports.txt gains an entry every
# ten minutes and progress_history.json a point every loop, for as long as a
# calibration runs, so tracking them would dirty the tree continuously for days.
# What matters from a run is in SUPERVISOR_JOURNAL.md and in the commits the
# supervisor makes when it intervenes.
code/progress_reports.txt
code/progress_history.json

# The compiled model. The supervisor rebuilds it on every intervention, so
# tracking a 1.4 MB binary here would add that much to history each time, for an
# artefact the source reproduces exactly.
code/hftf
# The build stamp beside the binary: the md5 of the source hftf was actually
# built from. It describes one container's binary and means nothing in another,
# so it belongs with the binary rather than in the history.
code/hftf.srcmd5
code/hftf_*

# THE LIVE LOG STAYS IGNORED, AND A COPY IS WHAT GETS COMMITTED.
# Tracking the file the running model holds open was a mistake: a
# `git reset --hard` replaces it, which unlinks the inode out from under
# the process. The model went on writing to a deleted file for half an
# hour while the visible log sat at a stale fragment. The supervisor
# copies it to code/calibration_run.log at each checkpoint instead, and
# that copy is what git carries.
!code/calibration_run.log
.claude/settings.local.json

# REGENERATED ON EVERY MODEL RUN, SO TRACKING THEM MAKES EVERY WORKER DIRTY.
# run_one_point.sh writes PARAM_HANDOVER.txt before each solve and the model
# rewrites B_SWEEP_RAW.tsv during it. While they were tracked, every worker
# came back with a modified tree, collided on push with the seven others
# pushing at the same time, and spent turns resolving stashes instead of
# solving points -- and an uncommitted change to one of them silently blocked
# the merge to main. They are inputs and byproducts, not results; the results
# are the point files.
code/PARAM_HANDOVER.txt
code/B_SWEEP_RAW.tsv
code/hftf_old

# run_one_point.sh serialisation lock (per checkout, never shared)
.run_one_point.lock
code/.run_one_point.lock
# Raw unpiped diagnostic run: hftf straight to a file with no grep or head in
# the way, used to separate 'the model stopped early' from 'the pipeline ate the
# output'. A per-container artefact like the binary, not history.
code/raw_control.log

# GENERATED VARIANT SOURCES AND THEIR BINARIES.
# make_variant.py rebuilds these from the master plus an asserted patch list,
# so committing 2.4 MB of near-duplicate Fortran would add nothing that the
# generator and the master do not already carry -- and a committed copy would
# go stale against the master silently, which is the one failure the md5
# sidecar exists to catch.
code/variant_*.f90
code/variant_*.f90.from
code/hftf_save
code/hftf_cf
code/hftf_*.srcmd5
